Among the $17{,}982$ primes $5\le p\le 200{,}000$, the congruence law of Theorem~\ref{thm:24} holds without exception.

\section{Discussion}

The channel framework holds for the non-abelian group $D_6$: every measured quantity is the Chebotarev shadow of an exact $D_6$ quantity. The abelian ceiling gives an information-theoretic form of the principle that congruences see only the abelian part of Frobenius. For $x^6-2$, the residue $\tfrac14\log_23-\tfrac16$ is concentrated on the coset $\{r_0,r_2,r_4\}$. The failure of information to translate into prediction has two causes. The $1/12$ floor comes from the commutator $r_2=[r_1,s_0]$, which glues the identity to $r_2,r_4$ under every abelian map. The zero gain of $p\bmod 3$ arises because the Bayes error sees only fibre majorities, while entropy sees whole distributions.

\section{Future work}

\textbf{Frobenius-group defect law.} For an odd prime $\ell$, the Galois group of $x^\ell-2$ is $\mathrm{AGL}(1,\ell)$. The identity fixes $\ell$ roots, the nontrivial translations fix none, and all other elements fix one. This suggests $H(T\mid G^{\ab})=h(1/\ell)/(\ell-1)$, where $h$ is the binary entropy, so the abelian ceiling captures a fraction of $H(T)$ tending to $1$. For $D_6$ this fraction is about $0.807$.
\textbf{General $x^n-a$}, whose Galois groups are subgroups of $\Z/n\rtimes(\Z/n)^\times$. \textbf{Effective Chebotarev} error bars for finite ranges. \textbf{Full cycle types} of Frobenius as finer read-outs.

